I’ve built CloudNotes, a note-taking app you rebuild on Azure over 10 phases, covering about 100 services. I checked that the lab scripts and Python files parse, but I couldn’t compile the Bicep here (no Azure CLI), so run az bicep build or what-if first.

Start with README.md, then follow LEARNING_PATH.md: a service-by-service table for each phase, a “Break it” exercise, and a checklist mapped to AZ-900, AZ-104, AZ-204 and AI-102.

Phase	Services
1. Foundations	RBAC, Policy, locks, budgets
2. Storage	Blob, Files, Queue, Table, SAS
3. Compute	VM, VMSS, App Service, Functions, ACR, Container Apps, AKS
4. Databases	Cosmos DB, SQL, PostgreSQL, Redis
5. Networking	VNet, NSG, Private Endpoint, DNS, load balancers, Front Door
6. Messaging	Service Bus, Event Grid, Event Hubs, Logic Apps, API Management
7. Security	Managed Identity, Key Vault, Entra ID, Defender
8. Monitoring	Log Analytics, Application Insights, KQL queries, alerts, Backup
9. DevOps	Bicep, GitHub Actions with OIDC, App Configuration
10. Data and AI	Azure OpenAI, AI Search, Document Intelligence, Data Factory, Stream Analytics
infra/main.bicep: deploys the core stack with passwordless access (managed identity), a dead-letter alert and diagnostics.
labs/01 to labs/10: CLI scripts to run line by line, not all at once.
app/api and functions/: a FastAPI service and a Service Bus-triggered Function that use Cosmos DB, Blob, Service Bus, Key Vault and Application Insights.
.github/workflows/deploy.yml: CI/CD without stored secrets.
Cost: set the budget alert in lab 1 first. Labs marked COSTLY (AKS, Redis, Firewall, Bastion, App Gateway) cost real money per hour, so delete them right after.
Not hands-on: Fabric, Databricks, Azure ML, Purview and Sentinel are portal walkthroughs only, because they’re expensive or need licences.
Azure OpenAI: it needs access approval and model names change, so check the Foundry model catalog before running that part of lab 10.

Next step: run az deployment group create -g rg-cloudnotes-dev -f infra/main.bicep -p infra/main.bicepparam, then start lab 1. Tell me which phase you want to go deeper on and I’ll expand it.
